In multilevel analysis, we often interpret the results through population-averaging. Population averaging removes all of the disturbances from the model by averaging them out. In a linear model this is straightforward. Consider, for instance, the random intercept model for level-1 units \(i\) nested in level-2 units \(j\) (e.g., individuals in countries): \[
y_{ij} = \underbrace{\gamma_{00} + \sum_{k=1}^K \gamma_{k0} x_{ijk}}_{\eta_{ij} = \text{Linear Predictor}} + \delta_{0j} + \varepsilon_{ij}
\] The standard assumption in the random intercept model is that the level-2 disturbances, \(\delta_{0j}\), have an expectation of 0 and that the same is true of the level-1 disturbances, \(\varepsilon_{ij}\). Hence, averaging across both units, we obtain \(\mathbb{E}[y_{ij}] = \eta_{ij}\).

